<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ceredigion</h1><p class="st-meta">420 CE – 730 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Early Welsh kingdom on Cardigan Bay, named after its legendary founder Ceredig, which grew into Seisyllwg in the 8th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Welsh tradition says the kingdom was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceredig-ap-cunedda">Ceredig</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunedda">Cunedda</a>, who came from the north to drive Irish settlers out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the 5th century, and that the land took its name from him. This story was recorded centuries later and is regarded with caution by historians, but Ceredigion as a district between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-dyfi">Dyfi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-teifi">Teifi</a> is ancient.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its rulers before the 8th century beyond genealogies. The kingdom covered the coastal lowlands and hills of modern Ceredigion, a region of small farms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-rearing, and its church centres included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llanbadarn-fawr">Llanbadarn Fawr</a>, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-padarn">St Padarn</a>, which became one of the leading monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 730 its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seisyll-ap-clydog">Seisyll ap Clydog</a> conquered the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ystrad-tywi">Kingdom of Ystrad Tywi</a>, creating an enlarged realm known as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-seisyllwg">Seisyllwg</a>. This later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a> and became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-deheubarth">Deheubarth</a>. The name survives in the modern county of Ceredigion.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ceredigion.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ceredigion" width="320" height="421"><figcaption>Image: Owj20 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Wales.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>420 CE – 730 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Seisyll ap Clydog</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ceredigion</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=575&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ceredigion" aria-label="Kingdom of Ceredigion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4590 346 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ceredigion lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4910 602L4910 602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=575&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ceredigion">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
